# --- Secrets and key material: never commit ------------------------------
# Node database encryption keys. Committing any of these compromises every
# ledger they unlock, including retroactively via the git history.
*.key
_keybackup/
_unsealtest/

# --- Ledger state ---------------------------------------------------------
# Node databases are per-operator runtime state, not source. genesis.json is
# deliberately NOT ignored: it is the canonical genesis, exported once and
# shared (see README).
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
covenant_sealed.bin

# --- Balances and account screenshots -------------------------------------
balance.png
balance_small.jpg

# --- Python ---------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# --- Versioned backups ----------------------------------------------------
# Superseded snapshots kept alongside the live file. Git is the version
# history once this is a repository.
*.PRE-*

# --- Runtime output -------------------------------------------------------
logs/
judge_queue/

# --- Vendored installers --------------------------------------------------
KrakenDesktopInstaller.msi

# --- Personal financial position: never publish -----------------------------
# holdings.txt is L's ACTUAL portfolio -- exact quantities and average buy
# prices. TRADING_POLICY.json carries the locked book value and the sleeve
# funding. Both were TRACKED until 2026-08-27, so they are still present in
# the existing git history: untracking them protects every FUTURE commit and
# does nothing about the past. Until that history is rewritten, any remote
# this repo is pushed to must be PRIVATE. See claude/GITHUB_IMPROVEMENTS.
holdings.txt
TRADING_POLICY.json
launch/*/holdings.txt
launch/*/TRADING_POLICY.json

# --- Launcher reports: outputs, not inputs ----------------------------------
# These are WRITTEN by the launchers into this folder. Tracking an output as
# an input means every launch dirties the worktree -- the same deadlock as
# hashing LAUNCH_CHECK.json in MANIFEST.sha256 (M48), one layer up. They are
# already on verify_bundle.py's OUTPUTS list; this is the git half of it.
# NB MANIFEST.sha256 stays TRACKED on purpose: it is a release reference, like
# a lockfile, written deliberately at release time rather than on every run.
DEPLOY_VERIFY.txt
LAUNCH_CHECK.json
NODE_RESTART.txt
launch/*/NODE_RESTART.txt
ONE_RUN.txt
ONE_RUN_cloud.txt
ONE_RUN_pc.txt
ONE_RETEST.txt
ONE_RETEST_gates.txt
ONE_UP.txt
PROBE_WIN_CONNECT.txt
GIT_SETUP.txt
ONE_RUN_ci.txt
GITHUB_PUSH.txt
GH_LOGIN.txt
GH_CODE.txt

# --- Transfer scratch -------------------------------------------------------
_stage/
_to_delete/
.fuse_hidden*

# --- ADDED 2026-08-29 by preflight_publish.py's G4 -------------------------
# G4 probes the ignore RULES with paths that do not exist (`git check-ignore`),
# so it tests what the file would actually refuse rather than what it looks
# like it refuses. On its first run it found these three uncovered, next to
# rules that already covered *.key and *.db. An ignore file nobody has watched
# refuse anything has not been tested (M31).
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.env
.env.*
.covenant-keys/
**/.covenant-keys/
secrets.*
*_secret*
*.seed
*.mnemonic
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*

# ...but a TEST ABOUT secrets contains none, and must be committed.
#
# THIS COST A RED CI FOR A DAY, 2026-08-29 to 2026-08-30. `*_secret*` above
# silently swallowed test_e1_secret_egress.py -- the regression suite for the
# credential-in-a-URL disclosure, whose every "secret" is a labelled fixture
# like "AIzaSyD-EXAMPLE-not-a-real-key" and which makes no network call at
# all. The file existed on the production PC, so the PC ran it and passed. It
# was never committed, so CI checked out a tree without it, the runner
# correctly reported it ABSENT, and every run went red from the commit that
# added it to the suite table onward. Green before, red after, and no defect
# anywhere in the code being tested.
#
# The rule matched on the NAME and not on the NATURE. A file called *_secret*
# is as likely to be the check that no secret escapes as it is to be a secret
# -- and the check is precisely the thing you most want in the repository.
# The same error turned up the same day in a guard that flagged twelve
# innocent files under ai_memory_system/ for containing "ai_memory": a
# pattern that cannot tell a thing from a thing ABOUT it.
#
# Tests and probes are never credentials. If one ever does hold a real
# secret, that is a defect in the test, to be fixed there -- not hidden by an
# ignore rule that also hides the test's absence.
!test_*secret*
!probe_*secret*
!test_*key*
!test_*env*

# --- corpus cache: reproducible, not committable --------------------------
# fetch46.py rebuilds these 46 public-domain Gutenberg texts (~17 MB) from
# claude_phil_CORPUS_MANIFEST.json. Keeping them locally is what makes the
# semantic model's vocabulary reproducible -- the model file's own
# `supersedes` block records an earlier model whose source is simply GONE, and
# weights nobody can rebuild are weights nobody can check. Committing them is a
# different question: they are re-fetchable from the manifest, so the manifest
# is the artifact and these are the cache.
docs/semantic/books/
**/books/
*.pkl
tokens.pkl
space_mc*.pkl

# --- exchange credentials and the balances they read ----------------------
# kraken_balance.py and coinbase_balance.py both REFUSE to read a credential
# from inside this folder -- that is the real control and it is in the code.
# These rules are the second layer: if one ever lands here by accident, git
# must not be the thing that publishes it. Defence in depth, not a substitute.
#
# The *_balance.txt files are not credentials. They are HOLDINGS -- what you
# own and how much -- which is nobody's business in a public repository, and
# the reason those scripts write them here at all is so Claude can read them
# WITHOUT the key. That argument stops working the moment they are pushed.
.kraken/
.coinbase/
cdp_api_key.json
*_api_key.json
kraken_balance.txt
coinbase_balance.txt
*_balance.txt
# Same data, machine-readable, written by the same two scripts for
# sync_holdings.py. It is HOLDINGS -- it must not be published either.
*_balance.json

# --- trader runtime config ------------------------------------------------
# trader_config.json carries `armed`. It is per-operator runtime state, and
# committing it could publish an armed configuration or, worse, arm a fresh
# clone by accident. covenant_trader.py rewrites a DISARMED default whenever
# the file is absent, so ignoring it fails in the safe direction.
trader_config.json
TRADER_HALT
# The scheduled run's log: portfolio totals, position sizes, orders placed.
# That is holdings by another name, and holdings are not published.
trader_log.txt

# restore-point snapshots of unlanded candidates -- never source
PRE-LAND-*/
pending-*.BACKUP-*/

# --- The private record: never publishable, by rule not by habit ----------
# CONSTITUTION.md II.4 says the private corpus is never published, in whole or
# in part, for any reason -- including a reason that seems excellent at the
# time. It names people who did not consent to being recorded and it carries
# one person's medical detail. Only its FINGERPRINT is published, in
# docs/SUCCESSION_ANCHORS.md.
#
# Until 2026-08-30 that rule was kept by remembering it. redundancy.py's
# principle check pointed out that nothing enforced it: the corpus lives
# outside the repository, so it had never been tracked, and "has not happened"
# had been standing in for "cannot happen". One stray copy into the tree is
# the whole rule gone, and it would be gone irreversibly -- a published git
# history cannot be unpublished.
#
# NOTE what is NOT ignored: ai_memory_system/ is the SOFTWARE and is public on
# purpose. The store is ai_memory/. One is code about the record; the other is
# the record. A pattern that cannot tell them apart is worse than none.
ai_memory/
ai_memory_backups/
covenant-memory-backup/
.covenant-keys/
audit.jsonl
guard_state.json

